sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-43> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Apart from history, nothing fundamentally distinguishes the axiom systems traditionally used in mathematics (logic included), and many other axiom systems, often simple ones, could serve as foundations for new fields of mathematics as rich as the traditional ones.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Traditional axiom systems are not fundamentally special; others could found new fields";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Implications for Mathematics and Its Foundations” (from p. 772), p. 816. Scope: axiom systems underlying fields of mathematics"
}
